A flag check should default to the **safe** behavior if the flag system itself is unreachable — never fail open into the new, unproven path:

```python
def get_recommendations(user):
    try:
        if flags.is_enabled("ml-recommendations-v2", user=user):
            return ml_recommendations_v2(user)   # new, riskier path
    except FlagServiceUnavailable:
        pass   # flag service down -> fall through to the known-safe path

    return legacy_recommendations(user)          # default-safe fallback
```

Notice the fallback runs both when the flag is off **and** when the flag service itself errors. A flag check that raises on failure, or defaults to "on" when the service is unreachable, turns your safety mechanism into a new single point of failure.
